// The configuration page can install an update, and the core runs as the
// unprivileged `ritornello` user with NoNewPrivileges=true: starting a unit
// through polkit is the mechanism, and this file is the authorisation. Third
// client of the pattern, after the power actions and the media mount.
//
// Scoped to ONE unit via action.lookup("unit"), like its neighbour
// 51-ritornello-media.rules. What that scoping buys here is worth stating,
// because it is not tidiness: `manage-units` without the check would hand the
// web UI — which has no authentication on the local network — the right to
// start, stop and restart sshd and the firewall.
//
// ritornello-rollback.service is deliberately NOT named here. It is reached
// only by OnFailure= on ritornello.service, so systemd is its only caller.
// Adding it "for symmetry" would let whoever reaches the page trigger a
// rollback, which is a downgrade of the running software on demand.
//
// What the unit behind this grant can do is bounded by the binary and not by
// this file: it forms exactly two paths, /usr/local/bin/ritornello-core and
// the plugins directory joined with a validated bare name. It reads no
// archive and has PrivateNetwork=true. So even a fully compromised core buys
// no root code execution through this grant — the polkit rules, the systemd
// units and the root-run mount helper are not expressible as targets.
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
  if (
    subject.user === "ritornello" &&
    action.id === "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
    action.lookup("unit") === "ritornello-update.service"
  ) {
    return polkit.Result.YES;
  }
});
